WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.140
- Money wins.

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:03.000
- You're corrupt and so is the world.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.580
- You were only with me to get to power.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.980
- Ah, their whole relationship's
just such a disaster.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.270
My name is Douglas Boneparth.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:10.680
I'm a certified financial planner.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.170
Today I'm gonna break down the finances

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.657
of the characters in
the show "Succession."

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:17.908
- I was just talking to my mom.

00:00:17.908 --> 00:00:18.741
- It's a great scene.

00:00:18.741 --> 00:00:21.780
- And she said, apparently,
he'll leave me $5 million anyway

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.580
so I'm golden baby.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.460
- It's actually a very bad
financial planning concept

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.470
to think that there's
going to be a windfall

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.870
in the future when you
really don't have an idea

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:32.520
as to when, how much,

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:35.250
or having any control
over that whatsoever.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.380
- You can't do anything with five, Greg.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:38.370
Five's a nightmare.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:39.203
- This is the best part.

00:00:39.203 --> 00:00:40.036
- Is it?

00:00:40.036 --> 00:00:42.540
- Oh yeah, can't retire.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:43.830
Not worth it to work.

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.710
- Now, let's say Greg
inherits $5 million today.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.380
Generally speaking,
inheritance from family members

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.680
or anyone from that matter
is received tax free.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.270
Well, how much can Greg live on

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:55.650
without running out of money?

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:58.140
The 3% rule is a convenient rule of thumb

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.200
because it demonstrates what
we could at least get out

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.450
of a sum of money by
investing it prudently.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:08.450
So 3% of 5 million would give
Greg about $150,000 a year.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.730
Now, that's a pretty good
living by anyone's standard

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:15.990
but he's sitting in his great
uncle's $63 million penthouse

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:18.900
on Fifth Avenue, and he
appears to be drinking a bottle

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.790
of Blanton's Single Barrel.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:24.007
If this is the lifestyle that
Greg has become accustomed to,

00:01:24.007 --> 00:01:26.220
$5 million isn't going to cut it.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.350
- Greg, do you think we
could figure this out?

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:29.806
You wanna transfer now?

00:01:29.806 --> 00:01:31.620
It's 40 K.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.500
- Here's the thing, if that's
a stainless steel Submariner,

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.420
nowhere near $40,000.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.300
But what about watches
being a good investment?

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.180
Yes, there are some watches
out there that do appreciate

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:43.560
in value over time.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.490
Will Greg make out well
with his $40,000 investment

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:48.510
in this watch?

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:52.020
Time will tell, but you'll
probably do better investing

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.880
in the US stock market.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.320
- If I try to say the truth.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.090
- My favorite interaction

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.340
between the two of them, I think period.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.570
So I love this one

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:01.683
- All my life,

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.280
I've been thinking a
little bit about money.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.350
- In the scene where Tom tells Shiv

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:09.060
that he often thinks about money,

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.910
he's really laying out
his financial background.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.350
We know his mother is.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:15.720
- A highly respected
attorney in the Twin Cities.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.120
- And a job like that
probably earns anywhere

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.660
from 180,000 to $250,000 a year.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.577
Far cry from billionaire status.

00:02:23.577 --> 00:02:25.080
(crowd chatting indistinctly)

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.210
- Well, this is delicious. Yeah, wow.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:28.680
- I'm very glad you like it.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:30.330
- Because it cost a pretty penny.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:33.090
- This whole scene is a great
way of showing the disparity

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:36.180
between the backgrounds that
both Shiv and Tom come from.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.190
If you're wondering what
the venue costs where Tom

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:40.020
and Shiv are getting married,
well, we looked it up.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:44.280
It's about $19,000 for two days.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:45.660
- It's massive.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.880
- Logan owns this castle and therefore,

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.280
has to not only maintain
it, but upkeep it.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:51.840
We see a scene in a room,

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.760
we got priceless pieces of art.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.620
We can only begin to imagine

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:58.560
how much this castle actually costs.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.300
- Come on, there's nothing
wrong with his body,

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:01.800
it's just suits.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.750
- Is he wearing a suit in that scene?

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.160
- [Speaker] You look
like a divorce attorney

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.150
from the Twin Cities.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:09.060
- I don't put it past Tom to
spend some money on a suit.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:10.560
He says he really likes his suit.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.040
Maybe spends $600 to
$1,000, had it tailored.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:14.873
Looks good.

00:03:14.873 --> 00:03:16.950
For most people, that's an expensive suit.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:20.040
We see Tom upgrade his
career into becoming the head

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.640
of ATN where he should have
no issues affording any type

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:24.810
of suit that he wants,

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.930
custom, off the rack,
designer, whatever it may be.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.670
- It's a really, really nice place.

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.078
- Thank you.
- Thanks.

00:03:31.078 --> 00:03:32.658
- Who'll get to keep it in the divorce?

00:03:32.658 --> 00:03:33.720
(both chuckling)

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.910
- So in season four, we learn that Tom

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:37.440
and Shiv may get divorced.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.290
It brings up a great question,
how are the assets going

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:41.123
to be divided?

00:03:41.123 --> 00:03:44.100
So when we think about the
assets in most people's lives,

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.740
that could get split up
during divorce, it comes down

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:49.740
to things like their retirement
accounts, checking accounts

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.030
and the family home.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:53.520
We have a feeling that they
live in lower Manhattan

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:57.210
in an apartment that costs
at least $5.8 million.

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:59.850
If Tom and Shiv do get divorced, a prenup,

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:03.330
which we know Tom likely
has signed, comes into play

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:04.830
and that will spell out exactly

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:08.010
what he does or does not get.

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:12.076
- She says that the prenup
is a little unconscionable.

00:04:12.076 --> 00:04:13.230
- Oh, hold it.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:16.680
That means it has to be so bad and so not

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:21.180
in Tom's favor for a
lawyer to use that term.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:24.120
- But she did notice that re infidelities,

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:25.254
there's no clause.

00:04:25.254 --> 00:04:26.610
- Yeah, we don't need to do that.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:30.060
- It is common to see
infidelity clauses in prenups,

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.700
but whether two people
should enter into a prenup,

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:34.800
that's really about what's right for them.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.650
You want to have a sense
of how they value money.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:40.860
What possessions or assets that
they have are truly theirs.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:42.450
- All the top attorneys
are conflicted out.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.250
- Conflicted out means
that there's a conflict

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.890
of interest with that
attorney representing her.

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:47.760
Spending a lot of money

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.000
on an attorney doesn't always
guarantee good results.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.090
But look, you usually get
what you pay for here.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:55.500
I can't help but to have this feeling

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:57.570
that Shiv will be okay

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:00.120
and it's really Tom that's
going to be, you know,

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:01.620
working hard to figure out

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:04.380
what he's entitled to, if anything at all.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.110
- I'm one of the executors
of your dad's affairs.

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:07.980
- In season four.,

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:09.990
Logan's death is the main event

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:13.230
that ultimately sets off the
name of the show, "Succession."

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:18.000
- And it appears, at a
certain point, undated.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.060
It was suggested that it
was your dad's wish that.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:21.893
- Okay

00:05:21.893 --> 00:05:23.700
- Kendall take over as CEO.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.110
- Okay, so classic.

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:26.250
There's a codicil here.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.320
That's an amendment to a will

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:29.940
or a legal document.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:31.380
This is actually pretty common.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:33.990
- He underlined, recently.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:36.570
- Underlined or crossed out?

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:40.200
- This only emphasizes the need
to have clear communication

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.850
with those you're putting in charge

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:43.500
of handling your estate.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:45.540
To ensure that this doesn't happen to you,

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.490
it's probably good form to make sure

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:51.510
that all estate planning
documents are in one place

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:53.250
and if you are working on any changes,

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:56.250
to communicate what those
potential changes might be

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:58.050
so you don't catch anyone off guard,

00:05:58.050 --> 00:05:59.820
like Logan here clearly is.

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:01.590
- [Lawyer] What else is in the?

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:02.423
Can you? I don't wanna...

00:06:02.423 --> 00:06:04.470
- Music, burial in the city, Catholic.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.150
- We're not just talking about assets,

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:07.530
the things that you own.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:08.970
We're also talking about children.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.250
And for more advanced
estate planning cases,

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:14.220
you have trusts or trusts
being born out of the will.

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:17.190
- He's got like a ton of
investment impressionisms, right?

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:20.370
Like he's got three Gauguins
no one's seen for tax reasons.

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:22.800
- And then you hear about
three Gauguin paintings

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:26.670
and those paintings can
fetch more than $100 million.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:28.290
So what's the tax planning around this?

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:31.800
Likely so that those assets
are excluded from the estate,

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.650
that there would not need to
be estate tax paid on that.

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:36.780
Generally speaking, it's never a bad idea

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:39.270
to have your basic estate
planning documents in place.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:42.120
This is your last will and
testament, powers of attorney,

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:44.689
healthcare proxy and a living will.

00:06:44.689 --> 00:06:47.700
(tranquil music)

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:50.550
Here in the show, we really
do get our first glimpse

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:53.190
at what Kendall might actually be worth.

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:55.890
Most of these shares probably
have a very low basis,

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:57.630
meaning they were gifted a long time ago,

00:06:57.630 --> 00:06:59.340
which means a significant portion

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:01.980
of that sale is going to be capital gains.

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:03.690
You might as well take about 1/3

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:06.513
of that gain off the table for the taxman.

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:09.690
So in this scene, we have Kendall going

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:13.110
to an investor meeting and
unboxing a pair of sneakers

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:15.060
from Lanvin, and he wants to show off

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.610
that he's got great style
walking into a meeting.

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:18.690
Now, I'm under the impression

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:21.450
that Kendall is spending his
own money to look the part.

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:23.610
Some could argue is he taking a deduction

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:24.810
to wear these clothes

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:26.670
because it has something to do with work.

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:28.890
Now, typically, that's
reserved for uniforms.

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:30.750
I wouldn't go quite calling a pair

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:32.880
of Lanvin sneakers a uniform.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:35.820
However, computers, transportation, meals

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:40.560
and expenses, wages, these are
all very, very typical things

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.009
that get written off
as part of deductions.

00:07:43.009 --> 00:07:44.760
(lively music)

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:46.350
In this scene, you get a really good look

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:47.610
at how the Roy family,

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:49.470
in this case Kendall, likes to travel.

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:52.080
This helicopter company
can take you from Manhattan

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:55.680
to the Hamptons for
about $4,000 plus taxes,

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:57.390
which if you are asking the question,

00:07:57.390 --> 00:07:58.950
is that deductible?

00:07:58.950 --> 00:07:59.880
Probably.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:01.950
So when it comes to deducting expenses

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:03.390
for business purposes,

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:06.510
there's usually not a limit
on the quality of things

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:09.900
but there are certain
thresholds, percentage of income

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:13.353
or revenue that would create
what's called an audit risk.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:17.550
And while Roman might
not be visibly as fancy

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:18.780
as his brother Kendall,

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:22.320
he clearly too likes to
travel in style and comfort

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:27.320
on his private jet costing about
$16,000 an hour to charter.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:31.230
And we see Roman pull up
in two Chevy Suburbans,

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:35.400
which would run about $800
a day to rent in Manhattan.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:39.660
Logan created this empire
and he clearly was set out

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:41.340
to leave this to his children

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:43.650
but his children are so affected

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:47.130
that he might not actually
get to see his own wishes

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:48.840
for them be carried out.

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:51.630
And this is all very typical in situations

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:53.880
where we're talking about
business enterprises

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:57.031
being passed down through family members.

00:08:57.031 --> 00:08:59.452
(pensive music)

